# A pattern ending in `/` matches only directories, and a symbolic link is not a
# directory to git — it is a file whose content is a path. So `node_modules/` did not stop
# `git add -A` from committing a *symlink* named node_modules, which is how
# tools/rehearse.js links the sandbox to this tree. The rehearsal then tagged a link into
# the operator's home directory and failed three minutes later, at the step that clones the
# tag. `git check-ignore` reports such a path as ignored, because it matches the pattern
# without looking at what is on disk, so the tool you would ask disagrees with what add
# does. None of the names below is ever a file this repository wants, so none of them
# carries the slash.

# Must never be committed: Pantheon admin credentials and backend secrets
secrets
*.env
.env*
# The admin credential captured at sign-in (server/admin-credential.js). It lives in
# var/ (ignored below) but is named here too, so relocating it can never publish a
# password-equivalent token by accident.
admin-credential.json

# One event's data, not source. These two are the public commitment and they MUST be
# committed and tagged — in the OPERATOR's repository, by tools/freeze.js, which adds
# them with -f for exactly this reason. This repository is the code; anyone can run it
# for their own event, and the target round, the cutoff and twelve real Pantheon ids and
# names belong to whoever did, in their own tree. Committing a developer's test event
# here would publish those people and prove nothing to anyone.
#
# data/*.example.json is the shape reference and IS committed.
data/protocol.json
data/roster.json

# One event's substitutions (PROTOCOL.md 11.6), for the same reason and one more: unlike
# the two above it is NOT frozen and must not be, since a player drops out weeks after the
# tag is made. It is published by being copied into events/final/lock.json, which is
# mirrored and timestamped, and archived by tools/end-event.js when the event closes.
data/substitutes.json

# The deliberate opposite: operational settings (PROTOCOL.md §4.2). Nothing in here can
# change or steer the outcome, and it may need to change mid-window — a dead drand
# mirror, a moved Pantheon. Keeping it out of the tree keeps the boundary physical, so
# nobody has to work out whether it was covered by the freeze. There are no secrets in
# it either; those are environment variables (deploy/README.md §2).
data/runtime.json

# Build and runtime artefacts
node_modules
# docs/seating-design{,.zh}.md, rendered into a module the client imports. Generated on
# every build from the markdown, which IS committed, so there is one source and it is
# the one an auditor reads. See tools/build-client.js.
client/generated
# SQLite state and scratch output. Losing var/ loses nothing that matters: every
# ciphertext is also mirrored into the repository as it arrives.
var
# Written by the draw itself: one event's outcome, not source. Same rule as the two
# frozen files above — it is published in the OPERATOR's repository, where that event was
# run, because that is where it proves something. A developer's test draw committed here
# would publish a dozen real Pantheon ids and be evidence to nobody.
#
# Unlike those two, nothing has to force-add it: server/mirror.js publishes results.json
# and everything under events/ through the GitHub contents API, which never consults this
# file. Nothing in the repository reads a results.json at the root either — every test
# writes its own into a temporary directory.
events
results.json
decrypted.json
my-results.json
independent.json
# Scratch names from the atomic write in server/mirror.js. One of these surviving means a
# process was killed mid-publish; the real file is either absent or complete, which is
# the point of writing it this way.
*.tmp

# Note: public/app.js, public/app.css and public/app.js.sha256 MUST be committed. The
# submission stage is where plaintext numbers exist, so the code running there is part
# of the freeze.
#
# public/figures/ is copied from docs/figures/ by the build and is also committed, but
# it is NOT part of the freeze digest: they are illustrations on the explanation page,
# and no byte of them reaches the code that seals a number.
